186159846 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "11be860b-a007-55a2-bb06-bac076846dea" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186159847 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{5}{8}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186159847 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186159847 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "390481e4-d8e5-5074-a53e-1a43032d2ec3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161706 #edb/ref 1069 "e-10372" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161706 #edb/ref 1071 "The variances of the random variables $X$ and $Y$ are $25$ and $36$, respectively. Given that $\\textrm{Cov}[X,Y] = 18$, find the correlation coefficient of $X$ and $Y$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161706 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "284f65cd-1288-56ed-80b7-f611b1464835" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161706 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that the correlation coefficient of the random variables $X$ and $Y$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\rho = \\dfrac{\\textrm{Cov}[X, Y]}{\\textrm {SD}[X] \\cdot \\textrm {SD}[Y]}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\textrm{Cov}[X, Y]$ is the covariance, and\n- $\\textrm {SD}[X]$ and $\\textrm {SD}[Y]$ are the standard deviations of $X$ and $Y$.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nSD[X] &= \\sqrt{Var[X]} = \\sqrt{25} = 5, \\\\\nSD[X] &= \\sqrt{Var[Y]} = \\sqrt{36} = 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting our data in the formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nρ &= \\frac{Cov[X, Y]}{SD[X] \\cdot SD[X]} \\\\\n&= \\frac{18}{5 \\cdot 6} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161707 #edb/ref 1069 "e-10373" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161707 #edb/ref 1071 "| $f(x,y)$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | $f_X$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $X=0$ | $0.1$ | $0.5$ | $0.6$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.3$ | $0.1$ | $0.4$ |\n| $f_Y$ | $0.4$ | $0.6$ | $1$ |\n\nThe joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is shown above. Given that\n$\\textrm {E}[X]=0.4\\qquad \\textrm {E}[Y]=0.6, \\qquad \\textrm {SD}[X] \\approx 0.4899, \\qquad \\textrm {SD}[Y] \\approx 0.4899$,\nfind the correlation coefficient of $X$ and $Y$. Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161707 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d132664b-222c-5dd5-a52b-2471d10fcb25" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161707 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that the correlation coefficient of random variables $X$ and $Y$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\rho = \\dfrac{\\textrm{Cov}[X, Y]}{\\textrm {SD}[X] \\cdot \\textrm {SD}[Y]}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\textrm{Cov}[X, Y]$ is the covariance, and\n- $\\textrm {SD}[X]$ and $\\textrm {SD}[Y]$ are the standard deviations of $X$ and $Y$.\n\nFirst, by applying the rule of the lazy statistician, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[XY] &= ∑_(x ∈ S_{X})∑_(y ∈ S_{Y})xyf(x, y) \\\\\n&= 0 \\cdot 0 \\cdot 0.1 + 0 \\cdot 1 \\cdot 0.5 \\\\\n&= + 1 \\cdot 0 \\cdot 0.3 + 1 \\cdot 1 \\cdot 0.1 \\\\\n&= 0.1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the covariance and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nCov[X, Y] &= E[XY] - E[X] \\cdot E[Y] \\\\\n&= 0.1 - (0.4)(0.6) \\\\\n&=-0.14\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correlation coefficient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nρ &= \\frac{Cov[X, Y]}{SD[X] \\cdot SD[Y]} \\\\\n&\\approx \\frac{-0.14}{0.4899 \\cdot 0.4899} \\\\\n&\\approx - 0.58\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161708 #edb/ref 1069 "e-10384" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161708 #edb/ref 1071 "${2(x + y),\\begin{vmatrix}0 \\le y \\le x \\le 1 \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$\n\nThe joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ for the continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is shown above. Given that\n$\\textrm {E}[X]= \\dfrac{3}{4},\\qquad \\textrm {E}[Y]= \\dfrac{5}{12}, \\qquad \\textrm {SD}[X]\\approx 0.1936, \\qquad \\textrm {SD}[Y]\\approx 0.2444$,\nfind the correlation coefficient of $X$ and $Y$. Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161708 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6562853a-696d-5cb5-a79d-76ff1a084d90" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161708 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that the correlation coefficient of the random variables $X$ and $Y$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\rho = \\dfrac{\\textrm{Cov}[X, Y]}{\\textrm {SD}[X] \\cdot \\textrm {SD}[Y]}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\textrm{Cov}[X, Y]$ is the covariance, and\n- $\\textrm {SD}[X]$ and $\\textrm {SD}[Y]$ are the standard deviations of $X$ and $Y$.\n\nFirst, by applying the rule of the lazy statistician, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[XY] &= ∬_{R^{2}}xyf(x, y)dxdy \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}[\\int_{0}^{x}xy \\cdot 2(x + y)dy]dx \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}[\\int_{0}^{x}(2x^{2}y + 2xy^{2})dy]dx \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}[x^{2}y^{2} + \\frac{2}{3}xy^{3}]_{y = 0}^{y = x}dx \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}(x^{4} + \\frac{2}{3}x^{4})dx \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}\\frac{5}{3}x^{4}dx \\\\\n&= [\\frac{1}{3}x^{5}]_{0}^{1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the covariance and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nCov[X, Y] &= E[XY] - E[X] \\cdot E[Y] \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3} - (\\frac{3}{4})(\\frac{5}{12}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{48}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correlation coefficient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nρ &= \\frac{Cov[X, Y]}{SD[X] \\cdot SD[Y]} \\\\\n&\\approx ((\\frac{1}{48}))/((0.1936) \\cdot (0.2444)) \\\\\n&\\approx 0.44\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162140 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048424 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162140 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050351 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162140 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186162440 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162140 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186162441 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162140 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding a Correlation Coefficient Given Variances and Covariance" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162140 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186161706 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162140 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "6b371f15-7541-5cd9-ac1c-e53fd3b8fc18" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162141 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186162442 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162141 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186162446 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162141 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186416506 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162141 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Correlation Coefficient of Two Discrete Random Variables" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162141 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186161707 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162141 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "c5fab7dc-3857-5840-a349-6d90e5f6e280" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162142 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186162443 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162142 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186162447 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162142 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Correlation Coefficient of Two Continuous Random Variables" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162142 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186161708 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162142 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "51a3f265-d2dd-5e62-931e-4bf6d7597ea4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162188 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186163010 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162188 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186163121 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162188 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186163122 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162188 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186163123 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162188 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162188 #edb/ref 1045 "The Correlation Coefficient for Two Random Variables" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162188 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186163010 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162188 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049206 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162188 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "4a097ed8-748d-54f4-a06d-fefa2b69e0d1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162440 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186159842 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162440 #edb/ref 1069 "q-146468" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162440 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $Cov[X, Y] = 5$, $SD[X] = 2$, and $SD[Y] = 4$, find the correlation coefficient of the random variables $X$ and $Y$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162440 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a81cd707-e6c5-5113-8dea-ede53eaaf544" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162440 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162442 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186416505 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162442 #edb/ref 1069 "q-146471" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162442 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162442 #edb/ref 1071 "| $f(x,y)$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.1$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ | $0.4$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.3$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ | $0.6$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $0.4$ | $0.2$ | $0.4$ | $1$ |\n\nThe joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is shown above. Given that \n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=0.6,\\,\\text{E}[Y]=1,\\,\\text{SD}[X]≈0.4899,\\,\\text{SD}[Y]≈0.8944,\n$$\n\n \nfind the correlation coefficient of $X$ and $Y.$ Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162442 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "86b78d29-f123-58f2-82ad-24e8b4486f2c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162442 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162442 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that the correlation coefficient of random variables $X$ and $Y$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\rho =\\frac{\\text{Cov}[X,Y]}{\\text{SD}[X]\\cdot \\text{SD}[Y]},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\text{Cov}[X,Y]$ is the covariance, and\n\n- $\\text{SD}[X]$ and $\\text{SD}[Y]$ are the standard deviations of $X$ and $Y.$\n\nFirst, by applying the rule of the lazy statistician, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[XY] & ={\\sum }_{x\\in {S}_{X}}{\\sum }_{y\\in {S}_{Y}}xy\\,f(x,y) \\\\  & =0\\cdot 0\\cdot 0.1+0\\cdot 1\\cdot 0.1+0\\cdot 2\\cdot 0.2 \\\\  & +1\\cdot 0\\cdot 0.3+1\\cdot 1\\cdot 0.1+1\\cdot 2\\cdot 0.2 \\\\  & =0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the covariance and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =0.5-(0.6)(1) \\\\  & =-0.1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correlation coefficient is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho  & =\\frac{\\text{Cov}[X,Y]}{\\text{SD}[X]\\cdot \\text{SD}[Y]} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{-0.1}{0.4899\\cdot 0.8944} \\\\  & ≈0.23,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162446 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186416504 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162446 #edb/ref 1069 "q-148368" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162446 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162446 #edb/ref 1071 "| $f(x,y)$ | $Y=-1$ | $Y=1$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.2$ | $0.5$ | $0.7$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.2$ | $0.1$ | $0.3$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $0.4$ | $0.6$ | $1$ |\n\nThe joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is shown above. Given that \n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=0.3\\,\\text{E}[Y]=0.2,\\,\\text{SD}[X]≈0.4583,\\,\\text{SD}[Y]≈0.9798,\n$$\n\n \nfind the correlation coefficient of $X$ and $Y.$ Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162446 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "972ce393-9eba-55fd-8649-cb51968b297d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162446 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162446 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that the correlation coefficient of random variables $X$ and $Y$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\rho =\\frac{\\text{Cov}[X,Y]}{\\text{SD}[X]\\cdot \\text{SD}[Y]},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\text{Cov}[X,Y]$ is the covariance, and\n\n- $\\text{SD}[X]$ and $\\text{SD}[Y]$ are the standard deviations of $X$ and $Y.$\n\nFirst, by applying the rule of the lazy statistician, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[XY] & ={\\sum }_{x\\in {S}_{X}}{\\sum }_{y\\in {S}_{Y}}xy\\,f(x,y) \\\\  & =0\\cdot (-1)\\cdot 0.2+0\\cdot 1\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & +1\\cdot (-1)\\cdot 0.2+1\\cdot 1\\cdot 0.1 \\\\  & =-0.1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the covariance and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =-0.1-(0.3)(0.2) \\\\  & =-0.16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correlation coefficient is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho  & =\\frac{\\text{Cov}[X,Y]}{\\text{SD}[X]\\cdot \\text{SD}[Y]} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{-0.16}{0.4583\\cdot 0.9798} \\\\  & ≈-0.36,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163010 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186163121 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163010 #edb/ref 1061 34624 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163010 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5afd6705-a870-5fc5-8b7c-544c322a2815" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163010 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186163294 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163121 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186163122 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163121 #edb/ref 1061 39086 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163121 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f053d13a-ebb8-5437-aa41-81a2459e4c6d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163121 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186162140 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163122 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186163123 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163122 #edb/ref 1061 39089 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163122 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ba6783eb-c401-5b3d-aa3b-85965b1f3255" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163122 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186162141 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163123 #edb/ref 1061 39123 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163123 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "083a778d-72e3-562d-b4fc-a52938de9b8b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163123 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186162142 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163294 #edb/ref 1064 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two random variables with covariance $\\textrm{Cov}[X, Y]$ and standard deviations $\\textrm {SD}[X]$ and $\\textrm {SD}[Y]$.\n\nThe **correlation coefficient of $X$ and $Y$,** also known as the **Pearson correlation coefficient**, is denoted $\\rho$ (the Greek letter \"rho\") and is defined as\n\n$$\n\\rho = \\dfrac{\\textrm{Cov}[X, Y]}{\\textrm {SD}[X]\\cdot \\textrm {SD}[Y]}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The correlation coefficient satisfies\n$-1\\leq \\rho \\leq 1$.\n- If $X$ and $Y$ are independent random variables, then\n$\\rho = 0$.\nThe reverse implication does not always hold. In other words, $\\rho=0$ does *not* imply that $X$ and $Y$ are independent.\n- Unlike the covariance, $\\rho$ is a *dimensionless* quantity. This means we'll get the same answer regardless of the units used to measure $X$ and $Y$.\n- Also unlike the covariance, $\\rho$ tells the strength of the correlation between $X$ and $Y$. We typically have the following rule of thumb:If $\\mid \\rho \\mid \\gt 0.7$, the correlation is **strong,** and the data generated by our two random variables closely follow a straight line.![](images/3f/3fd8b4e85bd76bbf78fdb2b135c49b3fd5e37fd9651dcae29d945d75aa45b905.png)If $0.3\\leq \\mid \\rho \\mid \\leq 0.7$, the correlation is **weak.** The data generated by our random variables follow a straight line, but many are far from the line.![](images/c1/c132ec90770bb84ad6c7dcc4b24773a923139c616f523ba0a23c1506c40c508c.png)If $\\mid \\rho \\mid \\lt 0.3$, then the correlation is **negligible.** No (linear) correlation exists between the data points generated by the random variables.![](images/9a/9afa80bfadc40b8b832b17dcea10d66d5a08955909d5bea66c7c010558b5ea19.png)\n\nLet's get some practice computing the correlation coefficient." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163294 #edb/ref 1065 5638 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163294 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "96cc345f-656a-5faf-a2cf-47946504320d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163294 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416487 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186416507 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416487 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186416508 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416487 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186416509 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416487 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416487 #edb/ref 1045 "The Correlation Coe